        <div id="reflex" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Reflex Deck Is, and Who Needs It</h2>
<p>
            "Reflex" is Freemotion's name for the cushioning deck on the t10.9b, the t22.9 and the
            Orangetheory treadmill. Dealers describe it as a two-sided, proprietary cushioning deck, and
            reviewers who have used it describe it as noticeably soft and springy compared with most
            commercial treadmills; TreadmillReviewGuru calls it the feature it likes most. Freemotion's own
            figure, reported by Athletech News, is a 29% reduction in impact force on the Orangetheory
            version. We have found no independent measurement of that.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cushioning is a matter of feel more than measured benefit. A softer deck can make long runs and
            hard intervals more comfortable, and some runners with sore joints prefer it; others find a
            very soft deck less stable for sprinting. The research does not show that deck cushioning on its
            own prevents injury. Our <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning guide</a> sets out what is known. The practical advice is simple: if you can, try a Reflex deck at an
            Orangetheory studio or a dealer before paying about $1,200 more for the t10.9b over the t8.9b.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 1-Step controls</h3>
          <p>
            The other Orangetheory feature is the 1-Step key layout: rows of direct speed and incline
            buttons, so you jump from a walk to a sprint pace with one press instead of holding an arrow.
            All three Freemotion treadmills have it. For <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>interval training</a> it is genuinely useful, and it is
            one reason studio runners like these machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-trainers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Freemotion Incline Trainers: The i10.9b and i22.9</h2>
<p>
            An <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer</a> is a treadmill whose deck rises much higher than the usual 15%. Freemotion's two reach 30%, and
            both decline to -3%, according to TreadmillReviewGuru and Garage Gym Reviews. Steep walking raises
            heart rate quickly with far less impact than running, which is why incline trainers are popular
            with walkers, hikers training for mountains and people who cannot run.
          </p>
          <p>
            The i22.9 is the flagship, with a 22-inch iFIT touchscreen and, according to TreadmillReviewGuru, a
            15 mph top speed. The i10.9b keeps the incline range with a 12 mph top speed and a console without a
            touchscreen; you pair a phone or tablet for iFIT. On 5 November 2026 Freemotion's home store listed
            the i10.9b at $9,999 and the i22.9 at $11,899.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same idea is available much more cheaply from Freemotion's sister brand. NordicTrack's <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X22i</a> and <a href="/nordictrack-x9i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X9i</a> incline trainers run the same iFIT content with an even steeper range, at a fraction of the price,
            with a consumer build and warranty. Choose the Freemotion for commercial construction and heavy,
            shared use; choose a NordicTrack if you are one person walking steep hills at home. Our list of <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the highest incline</a> compares the range.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the ceiling. An incline trainer's front end lifts a long way at 30%, and you stand higher
            on it. Measure the room's height against your own plus the deck's highest point before ordering.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What a Freemotion Asks of a Home</h2>
<p>
            Freemotion sells to home buyers, but these are commercial machines, and they bring commercial
            requirements. Before ordering, check four things.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Power.</strong> Both dealer listings for the t8.9b give 120 V and 20 A, and
            independent sources give a dedicated 20-amp circuit for the t22.9. Most household outlets are on
            15-amp circuits. An electrician fitting a dedicated 20-amp circuit is part of the cost.</li>
            <li><strong>Space.</strong> The t10.9b is 87.7 inches long and 34 wide on its dealer listing,
            and the t8.9b 81 by 36. Add safety clearance behind and to the sides.</li>
            <li><strong>Weight and access.</strong> The t10.9b weighs 487 lb and TreadmillReviewGuru gives
            the i22.9 about 750 lb. Ask who delivers, whether installation is included and whether the crew
            will handle stairs.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty for home use.</strong> The dealer listings quote seven years on the frame and
            drive motor, two on parts and one of labour. Those are short next to consumer brands that offer
            lifetime frame and motor cover, and terms on commercial equipment can differ between commercial
            and residential buyers. Ask the seller which terms apply to you, in writing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to putting a gym treadmill in a home</a> covers circuits, floors and delivery for any commercial machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is Freemotion sold on Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            We found no current Freemotion treadmill sold new on Amazon US by Freemotion or an authorised
            dealer. The one Freemotion we found listed there is the older i11.9 incline trainer, carded above
            with the checks to make before buying it. The other cards on this page are the closest machines
            Amazon sells from other brands. Buy a new current Freemotion from Freemotion's home store or an
            authorised dealer so delivery and warranty are clear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-nordictrack" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Freemotion vs NordicTrack: What the Extra Money Buys</h2>
<p>
            Because both brands run iFIT, the comparison most Freemotion shoppers make is with NordicTrack's
            top machines, such as the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2950-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 2950</a> or the <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X22i</a>.
            The content is the same. The differences are the hardware and the terms.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Motor type.</strong> Freemotion's treadmills use AC commercial drive motors rated at
            4.0 to 5.0 HP. NordicTrack's home treadmills generally use DC motors. AC motors are built for long, continuous
            use by many people; for one household, a good DC motor is usually enough. Our <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC vs DC motor guide</a> explains the trade-off.</li>
            <li><strong>Folding.</strong> Freemotion's treadmills do not fold. Several NordicTrack models do.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty.</strong> NordicTrack's consumer terms typically include longer frame cover,
            while the Freemotion dealer listings quote seven years on frame and motor. Compare the exact terms.</li>
            <li><strong>Power and weight.</strong> A Freemotion may need a 20-amp circuit and weighs far more.</li>
            <li><strong>Price.</strong> A Freemotion costs several times as much as most NordicTrack treadmills.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a single home user, the NordicTrack is usually the better buy. The Freemotion makes sense
            where the treadmill will see gym-level hours: a family of runners, a home gym shared with
            neighbours, a small studio or a fire station. If iFIT is not the point, other commercial brands — <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a>, <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a> or <a href="/true-fitness-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TRUE</a> — are worth comparing
            in our <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmills guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Used or Remanufactured Freemotion</h2>
<p>
            Because Freemotion sells to gyms and studios, used and remanufactured units are common, and the
            savings are large: one reseller in search results listed a remanufactured t10.9b at $4,399, about
            half the new price, though we did not verify that listing. Studio refits release batches of
            Reflex treadmills from time to time.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Know the history.</strong> A treadmill from an Orangetheory or a busy gym may have run
            many hours a day. Ask for the hour meter or odometer reading and the service record.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the version.</strong> Studio treadmills can be built to the chain's
            specification. Ask whether the unit is a standard t10.9b or t22.9 or a studio-specific model, and
            whether its console works without the studio's systems.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask about the warranty.</strong> A remanufacturer's own warranty is what you get; ask
            for its length and what it covers in writing.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan for power and moving.</strong> A used commercial treadmill still needs its
            circuit, and moving a 500 lb machine usually takes a professional crew.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills guide</a> and <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> cover what to inspect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Freemotion Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Choose the t8.9b</strong> if you want a commercial AC treadmill with a simple console at
            the brand's lowest price, and do not need the Reflex deck. <strong>Choose the t10.9b Reflex</strong> if you have run on an Orangetheory treadmill and want that
            deck and those controls at home without paying for a screen; pair a tablet for iFIT. <strong>Choose the t22.9 Reflex</strong> if you want iFIT built in, 15 mph and the brand's top
            machine. <strong>Choose an incline trainer</strong> if steep walking, not running, is the plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if:</strong> you are one person who mostly walks or jogs; you cannot fit a
            20-amp circuit; you need a folding treadmill; or you want iFIT at the lowest cost, in which case a <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>NordicTrack or ProForm</a> does the same classes. If you want no subscription at all, see our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
            This guide is about equipment, not health. If you are buying on a doctor's or physical
            therapist's advice, ask them what speed, incline and cushioning suit you; nothing here is medical
            advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Freemotion Treadmill</h2>
          <p>A Freemotion is a commercial machine sold to homes. Most problems come from treating it like a home treadmill.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the circuit until delivery day</h3>
              <p>The dealer listings we read give a 120 V, 20 A requirement for the t8.9b, and independent sources a dedicated 20-amp circuit for the t22.9. A treadmill plugged into a shared 15-amp outlet may trip it or run poorly. Ask the seller for the requirement and have an electrician fit the circuit before the treadmill arrives.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying commercial prices for iFIT you could get cheaper</h3>
              <p>Freemotion’s touchscreens run the same iFIT classes as NordicTrack and ProForm. If the classes are why you want one, a NordicTrack does that for a fraction of the price. Pay for a Freemotion for the commercial build and heavy shared use, not for the content.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the warranty matches consumer brands</h3>
              <p>Dealer listings quote seven years on the frame and drive motor, two on parts and one of labour, shorter than many consumer brands’ lifetime frame terms, and commercial terms can differ for residential buyers. Get the terms that apply to your purchase in writing before you pay.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying an ex-studio treadmill as if it were retail</h3>
              <p>Ex-Orangetheory and gym treadmills are common on the used market and may have run many hours a day, sometimes in a studio-specific version. Ask for the hour reading, service record and exact model, check the console works on its own, and get the remanufacturer’s warranty in writing.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Freemotion makes genuine commercial treadmills with iFIT: the t8.9b
          from about $6,000, the Reflex t10.9b at about $9,000 and the touchscreen t22.9 at $11,350 on
          Freemotion's home store on 5 November 2026.</strong> The t10.9b is the one to look at first if you
          want the Orangetheory-style Reflex deck and controls at home.
          </p>
          <p>
            Go in knowing what a commercial machine asks of a home: a 20-amp circuit on the listings we read, a
          deep room, a crew to deliver it and a warranty shorter than many consumer brands'. If iFIT classes
          are what you are after, a <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">NordicTrack</a> delivers the
          same content for far less.
          </p>
        </div>

