        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Who makes Freemotion treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Freemotion Fitness is the commercial brand of iFIT Health & Fitness, formerly ICON Health & Fitness, of Logan, Utah, which also owns NordicTrack and ProForm. It builds treadmills for gyms and studios, including the Reflex treadmills used by Orangetheory Fitness, and sells them to home buyers through a separate home store and dealers.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a Freemotion treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 5 November 2026 Freemotion’s US home store listed the t8.9b at $7,799, the t10.9b Reflex at $8,999 and the t22.9 Reflex at $11,350; its incline trainers were $9,999 (i10.9b) and $11,899 (i22.9). Authorised dealers listed the t8.9b from $5,999 and the t10.9b at $9,400. Remanufactured units sell for much less.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the Freemotion t8.9b and t10.9b?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The t10.9b has Freemotion’s Reflex cushioned deck and a 5.0 HP AC motor; the t8.9b has a conventional cushioned deck and a 4.0 HP AC motor. Both have a 21.5 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a 400 lb rating and no touchscreen, according to dealers and independent reviews. The t10.9b was $1,200 more on Freemotion’s home store.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Freemotion Reflex treadmill the Orangetheory treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Orangetheory uses Freemotion Reflex treadmills; Athletech News reported that Freemotion developed the OTF Reflex treadmill with the chain over about 13 years. The t10.9b and t22.9 sold to the public use the Reflex deck and 1-Step controls, but a studio unit may be built to Orangetheory’s specification, so a used ex-studio machine may differ from a retail one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do Freemotion treadmills need a 20-amp circuit?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The dealer listings we read give 120 V, 20 A for the t8.9b, and independent sources give a dedicated 20-amp circuit for the t22.9. Most household outlets are on 15-amp circuits, so plan for an electrician. Confirm the requirement for your model with the seller before delivery.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do Freemotion treadmills need an iFIT subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. The t8.9b and t10.9b run their own programmes and can pair with iFIT on a phone or tablet if you choose. The t22.9 and i22.9 have iFIT touchscreens; Freemotion includes a 30-day membership with the t22.9, after which iFIT’s classes need a paid plan. Manual running does not depend on iFIT.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a Freemotion better than a NordicTrack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is built for heavier use, with AC commercial motors and heavier frames, but it costs several times as much, does not fold, may need a 20-amp circuit and, on dealer listings, carries seven years on the frame and motor. Both run iFIT. For one household, a NordicTrack is usually the better value.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
